      "content": "{\n  \"schema_version\": \"domainspec.python-complete-verification-contract/v1-candidate\",\n  \"status\": \"candidate-construction-only\",\n  \"authority_effect\": \"none\",\n  \"extends\": [\"python-cli-integration-contract.json\", \"python-native-verification-contract.json\", \"python-verification-projection-contract.json\", \"reconciled-formalization-contract.json\"],\n  \"entrypoint\": \"The stronger explicit verify-project command dispatches Python to this complete verifier when given an exact existing output target, Python executable, private temporary parent and Lean executable. The artifact-currentness verify command and all TypeScript paths retain their existing behavior.\",\n  \"execution\": \"Join exact source replay, native compilation and imports, every selected Python semantic row, compiler-owned shared-source outcome agreement, exact output currentness, source-and-selection round-trip and finite structural Lean verification in one owned workspace. Native-only and semantic-only entrypoints remain incomplete and cannot return a whole-verification pass.\",\n  \"result\": \"Return pass only when every required Python verification-projection check passes and cleanup succeeds. Preserve observed failures and causal not-evaluable states. Pin and recheck Python, observed standard-library files, Lean 4.33.1, source bytes, generated bytes, formalization bytes and workspace identity.\",\n  \"claim_scope\": \"Python cross-generator behavior means Python native outcomes agree with compiler-owned shared source cases. R12 owns the full cross-language aggregate comparison. This verifier proves only finite structural references in Lean, not universal semantic correctness.\",\n  \"effects\": \"Explicit verification starts local Python and Lean processes and reads an existing selected output target. It removes only its exact owned temporary workspace. It does not publish output, execute production providers, change defaults, admit or release anything.\",\n  \"accepted_scope\": \"A Python HTTP target and Python cross-project HTTP composition adapter are intentionally outside the accepted product scope. Unsupported paths reject explicitly.\"\n}\n",
