      "content": "{\n  \"schema_version\": \"domainspec.resolved-artifact-plan-definition/v1-candidate\",\n  \"status\": \"candidate\",\n  \"authority_effect\": \"none\",\n  \"meaning\": \"An immutable in-memory build record joins selected existing native generators over one checked graph without changing their semantic contracts or output bytes.\",\n  \"selection\": \"Explicit versioned generator requests, one checked graph and composition boundary, registered exact field/action/workflow dependencies, and one common module root. No implicit legacy fallback.\",\n  \"identity\": \"Qualified domain selectors remain distinct. Native file identity is the existing generator-selected relative path within the selected layout, never an unqualified source name.\",\n  \"sources\": \"Retain selected definition and profile source bytes with content hashes and request-key bindings. No filesystem rereads during planning; old source versions remain separately selectable only where explicitly supported.\",\n  \"join\": \"Only identical file identity and exact content may deduplicate. Different content at one path, case-fold aliases, file/directory overlap and unresolved native leaf imports reject before any write.\",\n  \"surface\": \"Inventory static named native exports and imports from the bounded generated module grammar. This is not a second domain parser or proof of native typing. Native compilation and independent source observation remain explicit verification obligations.\",\n  \"effects\": \"Planning neither writes artifacts nor executes generated operations, policy targets, recovery, providers or publication. Candidate evidence grants no authority.\",\n  \"verification\": [\"checked snapshot provenance\", \"stale and forged selection rejection\", \"same graph and source pin closure\", \"deterministic request ordering and relocation\", \"source removal after snapshot\", \"exact content collision rejection\", \"native imports and exported symbols\", \"all thirteen TypeScript metatype joins\", \"independent native compilation and behavior\"]\n}\n",
